{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Mg2svwA6vVWt68gJGR4MM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# warmups — technique log (days 1-24; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n7\t- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13,\n8\t  sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal,\n9\t  \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL `%xx`, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML entities, \"Escape Room\" -> JSON\n10\t  `\\uXXXX`, \"Thirty-Two Below\" -> base32, \"URL-friendly vacation\" -> base64url with `=` DROPPED, \"wrong end\"/\"Back at\n11\t  You\" -> the sentence REVERSED, \"A met Z\" -> ATBASH, \"Caesar took a week off\" -> Caesar +7, \"punctuation went for a\n12\t  spin\"/\"Forty-Seven\" -> ROT47, \"Sixteen little symbols ... Dot dot dot\" -> MORSE over HEX, \"Sixteen guests. Five little\n13\t  a's and b's\" -> BACONIAN 5-groups whose values 0-15 are HEX nibbles, \"Three rails ... zigzag\" -> RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0\n14\t  (D18, first true transposition; the prompt's number was the key), \"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes\n15\t  first.\"/\"Little Things\" (D19) -> hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe` BOM, \"Same bar. This time the big one goes first.\"/\"Big\n16\t  Entrance\" (D20) -> hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM, \"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\"/\"Under Pressure\"\n17\t  (D21) -> GZIP inside BASE64 (`H4sI...`), \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little inflation.\"/\"Deflated Ego\"\n18\t  (D22) -> ZLIB (`78da`) inside BASE64 (`eNo...`), \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\"/\"Eighty-Five\n19\t  Alive\" (D23) -> ASCII85 in Adobe `<~ ... ~>` delimiters (one layer, plain UTF-8 inside; \"neighbors\" = the `<~` `~>` brackets,\n20\t  \"bigger alphabet\" = base16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85), \"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\"\n21\t  (D24) -> NETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,1:c,...` (DJB `<len>:<data>,`, every chunk ONE char so far; \"count\" = the length prefix,\n22\t  \"comma\" = the terminator, \"string\" = netstring). D1-D15 and D23-D24 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D22 two layers, the pun naming BOTH\n23\t  (every number/word in a prompt may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = an already-seen codec as the outer layer). D20 was the first day whose twist was a PARAMETER of the\n24\t  previous day's codec (\"Same bar\" = callback, \"big\" = the one knob turned), not a new codec.\n25\t  Families: D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation\n26\t  under hex, D21-D22 COMPRESSION under base64, D23 BIGGER BASE-N ALPHABET (Ascii85), D24 FRAMING / LENGTH-PREFIXED\n27\t  serialization (netstrings). Rhythm D19-D22: NEW family on day N, ONE knob on N+1 (LE -> BE; gzip -> zlib). D23 -> D24\n28\t  BROKE it (the predicted Ascii85 knob never came; D24 opened framing). Only a \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt signals a\n29\t  knob day; otherwise expect a new family -- lib cannot prebuild every family, so on an unknown shape read the blob's\n30\t  STRUCTURE (here `digits:char,` repeated) and ask which spec it is.\n31\t- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 83, 88). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, plain reversed text, Atbash, Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, UTF-16LE+BOM hex, UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, zlib-in-base64, `<~...~>` Ascii85, netstrings `1:o,1:n,...`, in that order so far; 118 x 24 on D24). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n32\t- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s and Mastery ~5-9 s windows (30 s on D1/D12); since D16 mostly `deadline: null` (D18 had\n33\t  30 s TT / 105 s Mastery again), `run_for_time: true` asks for a timed Hall of Fame attempt. The HARNESS kills solve.py at\n34\t  60 s regardless (D17 `solver_too_slow`; D21 Mastery 103 x 21 killed). Recent: D21 TT 4.1 s / Mastery 0.34 s solve;\n35\t  D22 TT 2.4 s / Mastery 108 x 22 28.2 s (solve 15.9 s); D23 TT 5.1 s (solve 0.75 s), Mastery 113 x 23 first run\n36\t  INCORRECT (all slug numbers wrong, see Pitfalls), fixed and re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 5.1 s, solve 1.2 s.\n37\t  D24 TT 4.6 s (solve 1.85 s), Mastery 118 x 24 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try, `deadline: null`.\n38\t\n39\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n40\t- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n41\t  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n42\t  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n43\t  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n44\t  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n45\t  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n46\t- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n47\t  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n48\t  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n49\t- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n50\t  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n51\t  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n52\t  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n53\t  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n54\t- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13-D15)** -- several FREE-TEXT transforms coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n55\t  reversal (12), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; not letters-only: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`/`` ` ``/`a`-`h`).\n56\t  Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on\n57\t  20/63 sets. Parse rule (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`): decode the strict lines, then try every assignment\n58\t  of the free codecs to the free lines, EACH USED ONCE per blob, keep the one whose joined text ends in a full 3-word\n59\t  `adj-noun-noun-NN` slug (rank 3-word above 2-word: D14 set22 `g-voaafk|urtle-60`), best bigram. Any new keyless\n60\t  printable-range shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n61\t- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a case/charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text. D16\n62\t  Morse: symbols split by ONE space, no word gaps, decoded symbols ONLY `0-9A-F` (UPPERCASE; `fromhex` takes it), ~11 B\n63\t  per char; Mastery line 16 = 14 symbols = 7 bytes. D17 Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb`\n64\t  (values 0-15, every group starts `a`), two groups per byte; read as Bacon-26 letters it gives only `a`-`p` (a middle\n65\t  layer). Parse rule: after any carrier decode, output `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` or exactly 16 symbols -> nibbles ->\n66\t  `fromhex`; digits-only -> char codes; else `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug.\n67\t  For any two-symbol group carrier: map to 0/1, fixed-width groups, values < 16 -> hex nibbles, < 26 -> Bacon letters\n68\t  (`warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`, widths 4-8, both polarities). In lib: `decode_chunk` Morse-hex and BACON-HEX branches.\n69\t- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n70\t  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n71\t  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n72\t  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n73\t  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n74\t  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n75\t  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n76\t  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n77\t  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n78\t- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n79\t  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n80\t  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n81\t  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n82\t  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n83\t  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n84\t- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- D21 single/TT = ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member\n85\t  (`1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03` = b64 `H4sIAAAAAAACA`); D22 = ONE std padded base64 string of a ZLIB stream `78da` -> b64\n86\t  `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNq`/`eNr` (3rd char follows the first deflate byte: match `eN`). Mastery lines 21/22 = 36- / 20-char\n87\t  padded fragments, each its OWN gzip member / zlib stream, side by side. Payload plain UTF-8; compressed bytes are not\n88\t  printable, so printable-gated b64 branches reject them and rot-N returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, took D22\n89\t  unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode -> sniff (`1f8b`, zlib, `BZh`,\n90\t  `fd377a585a`) -> decompress -> UTF-8; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n91\t  Widen the zlib test to RFC 1950 (`b[0]&15==8`, `(b[0]<<8|b[1])%31==0`, FDICT -> `zdict=` crib) and try raw deflate\n92\t  (`wbits=-15`); same rule for any wrapper (hex/b32/b85) and compressor (lzma-alone, zstd, lz4); re-run the full\n93\t  decoder on the inflated text.\n94\t- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, no newline/wraps/`z`, 141-143 B;\n95\t  Mastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, EACH with its own delimiters. The `!`..`u` alphabet overlaps\n96\t  every printable codec, so the delimiters are the cue (bare a85 collides with ROT47 -> slug-gate). Parse rule (`_a85`):\n97\t  `<~..~>` -> `a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else `a85decode` (z), `b85decode`, Z85 (translate to b85's alphabet); sniff\n98\t  magic, UTF-8 + slug gate; Mastery via `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` on the RAW line (Pitfalls, D23).\n99\t- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,` concatenated, no\n100\t  separators or newline, EVERY len = `1` (444 B for ~111 chars = 4 B per char); the payload's own `,` and `:` appear\n101\t  as data (`1:,,`, `1::,`), and spaces are data (`1: ,`). Mastery line 24 = the same 1-char netstrings over the fragment\n102\t  (`1:o,1:n,1:-,1:3,1:7,`, trailing `,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (D24 solver `_netstr`): cursor loop --\n103\t  `\\s*(\\d+):` at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end) right after, advance; accept only if the WHOLE text\n104\t  parses (else None, so plain text never matches); `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, fn)` for the Mastery line. Never\n105\t  `split(',')`. The loop already covers len > 1 and mixed lengths; unbuilt knobs: len in UTF-8 BYTES vs chars,\n106\t  hex/varint/binary length prefixes, tnetstrings (type tag `,#^!~]}` instead of `,`), bencode (`4:spam`, `l..e`),\n107\t  RESP (`$5\\r\\nhello\\r\\n`), HTTP chunked, `index:char` frames OUT OF ORDER, `count:char` run-length, nested frames.\n108\t- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n109\t- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, ..., 108 x 22 on day 22, 113 x 23 on day 23, 118 x 24 on day 24); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n110\t- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n111\t  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n112\t- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n113\t- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n114\t\n115\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n116\t| What you see | First move |\n117\t|---|---|\n118\t| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n119\t| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n120\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n121\t| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n122\t| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n123\t| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n124\t| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n125\t| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n126\t| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n127\t| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n128\t| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n129\t| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n130\t| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n131\t| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n132\t| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n133\t| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n134\t| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n135\t| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n136\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n137\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n138\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n139\t| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n140\t| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n141\t| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n142\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n143\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n144\t\n145\t## Day by day\n146\t**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n147\t`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\n148\tPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\n149\trot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\n150\tWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\n151\trefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n152\t\n153\t**Days 14-23** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n154\t+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM; gzip inside base64; zlib `78da` inside base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`):\n155\tCORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14 set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap\n156\tended in a 2-word slug (rank 3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal -> `register_free`. D16/D17\n157\ta carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s budget until `answer()`\n158\treturned early. D18 the anagram was cracked by rail r 2..N x every offset; lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried\n159\tsolver never routed to it; a TT start before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16`\n160\tcarried D20 byte-identical, and D21's magic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 (zlib) unchanged: write the GENERIC rule on day\n161\tN and day N+1's knob is free. D21 Mastery LOST its first run: an ad-hoc per-line BFS instead of the carried D20 lane\n162\tsolver -> 60 s kill. D23 (`base64.a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, ~36 s): Mastery 113 x 23 first submit had every slug\n163\tNUMBER wrong (+5 per digit) because `_a85_lines` pre-decoded line 23 in place and the joint free search ROT5'd the plain\n164\tline; fixed by letting `register_free(\"a85\", 23, ...)` claim the RAW line, re-submitted on the same attempt.\n165\t\n166\t**Day 24: String Theory** (\"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\" -- NETSTRINGS, a new FRAMING family).\n167\t- 444-byte `blob.txt`, one line, no newline: `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,1:w,...,1:,,1: ,...,1::,1: ,1:j,...,1:7,1:6,` -- the\n168\t  sentence one character per netstring (D. J. Bernstein's `<decimal len>:<bytes>,`). Pun: \"String\" = netstring,\n169\t  \"Count\" = the length prefix, \"the comma knows where to stop\" = `,` ends a frame that is found by COUNTING, not by\n170\t  searching for the comma (the sentence's own `,` and `:` are payload: `1:,,`, `1::,`). One layer, plain text inside.\n171\t  Triage previewed it but named no codec (its only hint, \"exhaustive listing: exact counts\", was a red herring).\n172\t- Single: first submit, start 13:16:29.6Z -> CORRECT 13:16:59.3Z (~30 s). A cursor loop (regex `(\\d+):` at i, take len\n173\t  chars, check the `,`) decodes it; the slug was read straight off the joined text and accepted as decoded.\n174\t- Solver: D23 lane solver + `_netstr` (whole text must parse, else None) as the FIRST whole-blob step (skipped for\n175\t  numbered Mastery blobs) + `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, ...)` for Mastery line 24. TT (444 B, same shape) CORRECT 4.6 s;\n176\t  Mastery 118 x 24 CORRECT 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try -- the D23 rule (let the codec claim the RAW line) held.\n177\t- Lesson: a structural codec (framing/serialization) has no charset fingerprint and lib had no prebuilt decoder; the\n178\t  shape `\\d+:.,` repeated (~4 B per char) IS the cue. Write such a decoder as a strict whole-text parser that returns\n179\t  None on any mismatch, so it can sit first in the pipeline without false hits.\n180\t\n181\t## GM answer conventions learned\n182\t- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n183\t- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-24; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n184\t- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n185\t- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n186\t\n187\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n188\t- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n189\t  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n190\t  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n191\t- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n192\t  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n193\t  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n194\t  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n195\t  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n196\t- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n197\t  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n198\t  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n199\t- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23, 113/113 sets wrong once): never rewrite a numbered line in place to plaintext before\n200\t  `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` -- the joint search has no \"identity\" choice, so a plain line gets a leftover free codec\n201\t  (digit ROT5 turned `el-46` into `el-91`). Register the codec instead and let it claim the RAW line. Check the joined\n202\t  Mastery sentence of one set by eye before a timed start; all-sets-wrong-in-the-same-field = one line's routing.\n203\t- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n204\t  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n205\t  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n206\t  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n207\t  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n208\t- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n209\t  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n210\t- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n211\t  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n212\t- FRAMED / LENGTH-PREFIXED text (D24 netstrings): the payload contains the delimiters (`1:,,`, `1::,`), so `split(',')`\n213\t  / `split(':')` corrupts exactly the punctuation the crib needs. Parse by COUNT with a cursor and demand the WHOLE text\n214\t  parse (strict -> None) so the step can run first without stealing other blobs. `count:char` (RLE) and `len:data`\n215\t  (netstring) read identically while every count is 1 -- a frame with N > 1 decides which.\n216\t- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n217\t  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n218\t- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n219\t  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n220\t  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n221\t  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n222\t- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n223\t  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n224\t  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n225\t- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n226\t  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n227\t  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n228\t- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n229\t  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n230\t  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n231\t- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n232\t- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n233\t- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n234\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f88f9f75-620a-469c-a76e-3699a2f1d5e0","uuid":"2b7d60ff-b149-4ad2-8dbd-29a09516350b","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:06:55.580Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/.claude/projects/-home-testpwn-huntress-2026/f88f9f75-620a-469c-a76e-3699a2f1d5e0/tool-results/buje7mhzl.txt","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-24; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13,\n  sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal,\n  \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL `%xx`, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML entities, \"Escape Room\" -> JSON\n  `\\uXXXX`, \"Thirty-Two Below\" -> base32, \"URL-friendly vacation\" -> base64url with `=` DROPPED, \"wrong end\"/\"Back at\n  You\" -> the sentence REVERSED, \"A met Z\" -> ATBASH, \"Caesar took a week off\" -> Caesar +7, \"punctuation went for a\n  spin\"/\"Forty-Seven\" -> ROT47, \"Sixteen little symbols ... Dot dot dot\" -> MORSE over HEX, \"Sixteen guests. Five little\n  a's and b's\" -> BACONIAN 5-groups whose values 0-15 are HEX nibbles, \"Three rails ... zigzag\" -> RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0\n  (D18, first true transposition; the prompt's number was the key), \"Two bytes walk into a bar. The little one goes\n  first.\"/\"Little Things\" (D19) -> hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe` BOM, \"Same bar. This time the big one goes first.\"/\"Big\n  Entrance\" (D20) -> hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM, \"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\"/\"Under Pressure\"\n  (D21) -> GZIP inside BASE64 (`H4sI...`), \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little inflation.\"/\"Deflated Ego\"\n  (D22) -> ZLIB (`78da`) inside BASE64 (`eNo...`), \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\"/\"Eighty-Five\n  Alive\" (D23) -> ASCII85 in Adobe `<~ ... ~>` delimiters (one layer, plain UTF-8 inside; \"neighbors\" = the `<~` `~>` brackets,\n  \"bigger alphabet\" = base16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 85), \"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\"\n  (D24) -> NETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,1:c,...` (DJB `<len>:<data>,`, every chunk ONE char so far; \"count\" = the length prefix,\n  \"comma\" = the terminator, \"string\" = netstring). D1-D15 and D23-D24 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D22 two layers, the pun naming BOTH\n  (every number/word in a prompt may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = an already-seen codec as the outer layer). D20 was the first day whose twist was a PARAMETER of the\n  previous day's codec (\"Same bar\" = callback, \"big\" = the one knob turned), not a new codec.\n  Families: D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation\n  under hex, D21-D22 COMPRESSION under base64, D23 BIGGER BASE-N ALPHABET (Ascii85), D24 FRAMING / LENGTH-PREFIXED\n  serialization (netstrings). Rhythm D19-D22: NEW family on day N, ONE knob on N+1 (LE -> BE; gzip -> zlib). D23 -> D24\n  BROKE it (the predicted Ascii85 knob never came; D24 opened framing). Only a \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt signals a\n  knob day; otherwise expect a new family -- lib cannot prebuild every family, so on an unknown shape read the blob's\n  STRUCTURE (here `digits:char,` repeated) and ask which spec it is.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 83, 88). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, plain reversed text, Atbash, Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, UTF-16LE+BOM hex, UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, zlib-in-base64, `<~...~>` Ascii85, netstrings `1:o,1:n,...`, in that order so far; 118 x 24 on D24). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s and Mastery ~5-9 s windows (30 s on D1/D12); since D16 mostly `deadline: null` (D18 had\n  30 s TT / 105 s Mastery again), `run_for_time: true` asks for a timed Hall of Fame attempt. The HARNESS kills solve.py at\n  60 s regardless (D17 `solver_too_slow`; D21 Mastery 103 x 21 killed). Recent: D21 TT 4.1 s / Mastery 0.34 s solve;\n  D22 TT 2.4 s / Mastery 108 x 22 28.2 s (solve 15.9 s); D23 TT 5.1 s (solve 0.75 s), Mastery 113 x 23 first run\n  INCORRECT (all slug numbers wrong, see Pitfalls), fixed and re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 5.1 s, solve 1.2 s.\n  D24 TT 4.6 s (solve 1.85 s), Mastery 118 x 24 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try, `deadline: null`.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13-D15)** -- several FREE-TEXT transforms coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n  reversal (12), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; not letters-only: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`/`` ` ``/`a`-`h`).\n  Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on\n  20/63 sets. Parse rule (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`): decode the strict lines, then try every assignment\n  of the free codecs to the free lines, EACH USED ONCE per blob, keep the one whose joined text ends in a full 3-word\n  `adj-noun-noun-NN` slug (rank 3-word above 2-word: D14 set22 `g-voaafk|urtle-60`), best bigram. Any new keyless\n  printable-range shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a case/charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text. D16\n  Morse: symbols split by ONE space, no word gaps, decoded symbols ONLY `0-9A-F` (UPPERCASE; `fromhex` takes it), ~11 B\n  per char; Mastery line 16 = 14 symbols = 7 bytes. D17 Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb`\n  (values 0-15, every group starts `a`), two groups per byte; read as Bacon-26 letters it gives only `a`-`p` (a middle\n  layer). Parse rule: after any carrier decode, output `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` or exactly 16 symbols -> nibbles ->\n  `fromhex`; digits-only -> char codes; else `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug.\n  For any two-symbol group carrier: map to 0/1, fixed-width groups, values < 16 -> hex nibbles, < 26 -> Bacon letters\n  (`warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`, widths 4-8, both polarities). In lib: `decode_chunk` Morse-hex and BACON-HEX branches.\n- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- D21 single/TT = ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member\n  (`1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03` = b64 `H4sIAAAAAAACA`); D22 = ONE std padded base64 string of a ZLIB stream `78da` -> b64\n  `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNq`/`eNr` (3rd char follows the first deflate byte: match `eN`). Mastery lines 21/22 = 36- / 20-char\n  padded fragments, each its OWN gzip member / zlib stream, side by side. Payload plain UTF-8; compressed bytes are not\n  printable, so printable-gated b64 branches reject them and rot-N returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, took D22\n  unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode -> sniff (`1f8b`, zlib, `BZh`,\n  `fd377a585a`) -> decompress -> UTF-8; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n  Widen the zlib test to RFC 1950 (`b[0]&15==8`, `(b[0]<<8|b[1])%31==0`, FDICT -> `zdict=` crib) and try raw deflate\n  (`wbits=-15`); same rule for any wrapper (hex/b32/b85) and compressor (lzma-alone, zstd, lz4); re-run the full\n  decoder on the inflated text.\n- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, no newline/wraps/`z`, 141-143 B;\n  Mastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, EACH with its own delimiters. The `!`..`u` alphabet overlaps\n  every printable codec, so the delimiters are the cue (bare a85 collides with ROT47 -> slug-gate). Parse rule (`_a85`):\n  `<~..~>` -> `a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else `a85decode` (z), `b85decode`, Z85 (translate to b85's alphabet); sniff\n  magic, UTF-8 + slug gate; Mastery via `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` on the RAW line (Pitfalls, D23).\n- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,` concatenated, no\n  separators or newline, EVERY len = `1` (444 B for ~111 chars = 4 B per char); the payload's own `,` and `:` appear\n  as data (`1:,,`, `1::,`), and spaces are data (`1: ,`). Mastery line 24 = the same 1-char netstrings over the fragment\n  (`1:o,1:n,1:-,1:3,1:7,`, trailing `,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (D24 solver `_netstr`): cursor loop --\n  `\\s*(\\d+):` at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end) right after, advance; accept only if the WHOLE text\n  parses (else None, so plain text never matches); `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, fn)` for the Mastery line. Never\n  `split(',')`. The loop already covers len > 1 and mixed lengths; unbuilt knobs: len in UTF-8 BYTES vs chars,\n  hex/varint/binary length prefixes, tnetstrings (type tag `,#^!~]}` instead of `,`), bencode (`4:spam`, `l..e`),\n  RESP (`$5\\r\\nhello\\r\\n`), HTTP chunked, `index:char` frames OUT OF ORDER, `count:char` run-length, nested frames.\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, ..., 108 x 22 on day 22, 113 x 23 on day 23, 118 x 24 on day 24); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\nPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\nrot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\nWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\nrefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n\n**Days 14-23** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM; gzip inside base64; zlib `78da` inside base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`):\nCORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14 set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap\nended in a 2-word slug (rank 3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal -> `register_free`. D16/D17\na carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s budget until `answer()`\nreturned early. D18 the anagram was cracked by rail r 2..N x every offset; lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried\nsolver never routed to it; a TT start before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16`\ncarried D20 byte-identical, and D21's magic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 (zlib) unchanged: write the GENERIC rule on day\nN and day N+1's knob is free. D21 Mastery LOST its first run: an ad-hoc per-line BFS instead of the carried D20 lane\nsolver -> 60 s kill. D23 (`base64.a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, ~36 s): Mastery 113 x 23 first submit had every slug\nNUMBER wrong (+5 per digit) because `_a85_lines` pre-decoded line 23 in place and the joint free search ROT5'd the plain\nline; fixed by letting `register_free(\"a85\", 23, ...)` claim the RAW line, re-submitted on the same attempt.\n\n**Day 24: String Theory** (\"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\" -- NETSTRINGS, a new FRAMING family).\n- 444-byte `blob.txt`, one line, no newline: `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,1:w,...,1:,,1: ,...,1::,1: ,1:j,...,1:7,1:6,` -- the\n  sentence one character per netstring (D. J. Bernstein's `<decimal len>:<bytes>,`). Pun: \"String\" = netstring,\n  \"Count\" = the length prefix, \"the comma knows where to stop\" = `,` ends a frame that is found by COUNTING, not by\n  searching for the comma (the sentence's own `,` and `:` are payload: `1:,,`, `1::,`). One layer, plain text inside.\n  Triage previewed it but named no codec (its only hint, \"exhaustive listing: exact counts\", was a red herring).\n- Single: first submit, start 13:16:29.6Z -> CORRECT 13:16:59.3Z (~30 s). A cursor loop (regex `(\\d+):` at i, take len\n  chars, check the `,`) decodes it; the slug was read straight off the joined text and accepted as decoded.\n- Solver: D23 lane solver + `_netstr` (whole text must parse, else None) as the FIRST whole-blob step (skipped for\n  numbered Mastery blobs) + `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, ...)` for Mastery line 24. TT (444 B, same shape) CORRECT 4.6 s;\n  Mastery 118 x 24 CORRECT 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try -- the D23 rule (let the codec claim the RAW line) held.\n- Lesson: a structural codec (framing/serialization) has no charset fingerprint and lib had no prebuilt decoder; the\n  shape `\\d+:.,` repeated (~4 B per char) IS the cue. Write such a decoder as a strict whole-text parser that returns\n  None on any mismatch, so it can sit first in the pipeline without false hits.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-24; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23, 113/113 sets wrong once): never rewrite a numbered line in place to plaintext before\n  `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` -- the joint search has no \"identity\" choice, so a plain line gets a leftover free codec\n  (digit ROT5 turned `el-46` into `el-91`). Register the codec instead and let it claim the RAW line. Check the joined\n  Mastery sentence of one set by eye before a timed start; all-sets-wrong-in-the-same-field = one line's routing.\n- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n- FRAMED / LENGTH-PREFIXED text (D24 netstrings): the payload contains the delimiters (`1:,,`, `1::,`), so `split(',')`\n  / `split(':')` corrupts exactly the punctuation the crib needs. Parse by COUNT with a cursor and demand the WHOLE text\n  parse (strict -> None) so the step can run first without stealing other blobs. `count:char` (RLE) and `len:data`\n  (netstring) read identically while every count is 1 -- a frame with N > 1 decides which.\n- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n- Don't over-