{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgP1Rw7TkNVLe1HJZwujM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A5836hCdE98vuihgbGc3mw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/testpwn/huntress-2026; cat > $TMPDIR/ed_tech.py <<'PYEOF'\nimport sys\np='reference/techniques/warmups.md'\nt=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(a)==1,(t.count(a),a[:80])\n    t=t.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"technique log (days 1-24;\",\"technique log (days 1-25;\")\nrep(\"\"\"  \"comma\" = the terminator, \"string\" = netstring). D1-D15 and D23-D24 one layer;\"\"\",\n\"\"\"  \"comma\" = the terminator, \"string\" = netstring), \"Every character has a point. A code point, anyway.\"/\"You Have a\n  Point\" (D25) -> Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...` (one token per char; the D9 `\\\\uXXXX` idea in Unicode-standard\n  notation). D1-D15 and D23-D25 one layer;\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"  serialization (netstrings). Rhythm D19-D22:\"\"\",\"\"\"  serialization (netstrings), D25 CHARACTER NOTATION (code points; back in the D7-D9 escape family). Rhythm D19-D22:\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"  BROKE it (the predicted Ascii85 knob never came; D24 opened framing).\"\"\",\"\"\"  BROKE it (the predicted Ascii85 knob never came; D24 opened framing; D25 jumped again, to code points).\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"`<~...~>` Ascii85, netstrings `1:o,1:n,...`, in that order so far; 118 x 24 on D24)\"\"\",\"\"\"`<~...~>` Ascii85, netstrings `1:o,1:n,...`, `U+006C U+0065 ...` code points, in that order so far; 123 x 25 on D25)\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"  D24 TT 4.6 s (solve 1.85 s), Mastery 118 x 24 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try, `deadline: null`.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"  D24 TT 4.6 s (solve 1.85 s), Mastery 118 x 24 4.5 s (solve 1.6 s), both first try, `deadline: null`. D25 TT 1.1 s\n  (solve 0.21 s) first try; Mastery 123 x 25 first submit INCORRECT (every NN wrong, the D23 bug again), fixed and\n  re-submitted on the SAME attempt: 2.1 s (solve 0.76 s), `deadline: null`.\"\"\")\n# variant axes\nrep(\"\"\"  `\\\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\\\xNN`.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"  `\\\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\\\xNN`.\n- **Code-point notation (D9, D25)** -- the same \"one token per char\" idea in different dress. Values seen: D9 JSON\n  `\"\\\\u00XX\"` (quoted, lowercase); D25 single/TT `U+XXXX` UPPERCASE hex, exactly 4 digits, ONE space between tokens, no\n  newline (762 B = 7 B per char; spaces are `U+0020`, so no literal space is payload); Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens per\n  fragment (`U+006C U+0065 U+002D U+0035 U+0032`), 123/123 sets. Parse rule (D25 `_uplus`, lib\n  `warmups_codecs.uplus_dec`): the WHOLE text must be tokens `(?:U\\\\+|u\\\\+|\\\\\\\\u|\\\\\\\\U|%u|0x)[0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8}` split by\n  whitespace/`,;` -> `chr(int(h,16))` (2-8 digits covers astral `U+1F600`, `\\\\U0001F600`); anything else -> None.\n  Unbuilt knobs: braces `\\\\u{4E}`/`\\\\x{4E}`, decimal/octal code points, names, offset blocks (atlas gap list).\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"- **Line wrapper / positional routing** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in\n  number order. Line k is decoded by `decode._positional` with day k's REGISTERED codec; a line whose day has no\n  registered codec goes to `_unknown_line_cands`, which re-\"decodes\" text that is already plain (D25: `le-52` -> `-07`).\n  Rule: every new day = `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line, even when a whole-blob step decodes single/TT.\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"113 x 23 on day 23, 118 x 24 on day 24);\"\"\",\"\"\"113 x 23 on day 23, 118 x 24 on day 24, 123 x 25 on day 25);\"\"\")\n# recognition table\nrep(\"\"\"| peel finds nothing |\"\"\",\"\"\"| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n| peel finds nothing |\"\"\")\n# day by day: compress D24 into block, add D25\ni=t.index(\"**Days 14-23**\"); j=t.index(\"## GM answer conventions learned\")\nt=t[:i]+\"\"\"**Days 14-24** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM; gzip inside base64; zlib `78da` inside base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`;\nnetstrings `1:N,1:i,...`): CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14 set22 went MISSING\nwhen a rev/Atbash swap ended in a 2-word slug (rank 3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal ->\n`register_free`. D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s\nbudget until `answer()` returned early. D18 the anagram was cracked by rail r 2..N x every offset; lib's `rail_solve`\nexisted but the carried solver never routed to it; a TT start before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19's\nBOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 byte-identical, and D21's magic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 (zlib) unchanged: write\nthe GENERIC rule on day N and day N+1's knob is free. D21 Mastery LOST its first run: an ad-hoc per-line BFS instead of\nthe carried D20 lane solver -> 60 s kill. D23 Mastery 113 x 23 first submit had every slug NUMBER wrong (+5 per digit):\n`_a85_lines` pre-decoded line 23 in place and the joint free search ROT5'd the plain line; fixed by letting\n`register_free(\"a85\", 23, ...)` claim the RAW line. D24 (444 B, `<len>:<data>,` per char, the sentence's own `,`/`:`\nframed as `1:,,`/`1::,`; ~30 s): a strict whole-text cursor parser (`_netstr`, None on any mismatch) sits first in the\npipeline; a structural codec has no charset fingerprint -- the repeating unit `\\\\d+:.,` IS the cue.\n\n**Day 25: You Have a Point** (\"Every character has a point. A code point, anyway.\" -- Unicode CODE POINTS).\n- 762-byte `blob.txt`, one line, no newline: `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 U+0065 U+0020 U+0077 ...` -- one `U+XXXX` token\n  (uppercase, 4 hex digits) per character of the 109-char sentence, single spaces between. One layer. Pun: \"point\" =\n  code point, \"every character\" = one token each. Triage previewed it; lib already had `uplus_dec` (prebuilt\n  `tgt:unicode-codepoint-notation`, row 22 of the atlas, rated \"med\").\n- Single: first submit, start 13:00:22.3Z -> CORRECT 13:00:49.2Z (~27 s); slug accepted exactly as decoded.\n- Solver: D24 lane solver + `_uplus` whole-blob step. TT CORRECT 1.1 s first try. Mastery 123 x 25 first submit\n  INCORRECT on all 123 sets -- slug words right, NN wrong (`-07`/`-22`/`-02` for `-52`/`-77`/`-57`): `_uplus_lines`\n  pre-decoded line 25 to plain `le-52`, no day-25 codec was registered, so `decode._positional` treated the last line\n  as UNKNOWN and `_unknown_line_cands` \"re-decoded\" the plain text. Generalize worker (~3 min) left line 25 raw and\n  added `register_free(\"uplus\", 25, ...)`; re-submitted on the same attempt -> CORRECT 2.1 s (solve 0.76 s).\n- Lesson: the D23 trap struck AGAIN through a different code path. A new day's Mastery codec must be REGISTERED by day\n  number, never applied as a line pre-pass; before any timed start, print one joined Mastery sentence and compare the\n  slug's NN with the raw last line by eye (here the raw line literally spelled `5` `2`).\n\n\"\"\"+t[j:]\nrep(\"(days 1-24; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs\",\"(days 1-25; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs\")\nrep(\"\"\"- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23, 113/113 sets wrong once):\"\"\",\"\"\"- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23 113/113 and D25 123/123 sets wrong once each):\